# SPA fallback, for static hosts that read this file (Cloudflare Pages, Netlify).
#
# `src/main.tsx` reads the pathname and mounts the wind map, the marketplace,
# the community, the AI page or a riding place's spot page, so /market,
# /market/sell, /market/listing/:id, /community, /community/t/:id and
# /spot/:id, /stars and /spots are real URLs with no file behind them. A host that 404s
# them breaks every direct load, refresh and shared link into either section.
#
# These twelve rules are exactly what that entry point branches on, and nothing
# else. A blanket /* rule would be wrong here: this origin also serves the
# ingest output under /weather and /marine, and every loader in the product
# gates on `!response.ok` and then parses what it got — a missing snapshot
# (src/App.tsx), a pruned frame (force5/src/weather.ts), a model that has not
# published yet (force5/src/manifests.ts, where 404 is a normal state) and an
# API call (market/web/api.ts). Answering 200 with an HTML body turns each of
# those actionable messages into a parse error. deploy/local/Caddyfile keeps
# /weather/* and /marine/* out of its `try_files` for the same reason.
#
# The target is `/`, not `/index.html`: Cloudflare Pages answers /index.html
# with a 308 to `/`, so a rule rewriting to it sent every exact section URL
# (/market, /stars …) to the map instead. public/404.html keeps Pages from its
# own single-page fallback, which would answer every unknown path with a 200.
#
# Hosts that do not read this file ignore it: `vite preview` has the fallback
# built in, and the Caddyfile does it with `try_files {path} /index.html`.
/market         /  200
/market/*       /  200
/community      /  200
/community/*    /  200
/ai             /  200
/ai/*           /  200
/spot           /  200
/spot/*         /  200
/stars          /  200
/stars/*        /  200
/spots          /  200
/spots/*        /  200
